      {
        "day_number": 128,
        "title": "May 7",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Great multitudes followed him, and he healed them all.”</em></p>\n<p>Matthew 12:15</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Matthew 12:15 describes crowds bringing people to Jesus and Jesus healing them. Spurgeon's original imagery treats illnesses and disabilities as repulsive monsters and then uses them as symbols of sin; that language is omitted. The Gospel instead shows Jesus receiving sick and disabled people without disgust. Pray for healing and accessible care, respect appropriate medical treatment, and honor each person's dignity whether or not healing comes as hoped.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Jesus says to him, Rise, take up your bed, and walk.”</em></p>\n<p>John 5:8</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> In John 5:8 Jesus speaks directly to a man who has waited many years for help. Spurgeon's application uses the man's disability as an analogy for spiritual failure and suggests that delayed healing reflects unbelief; that framing is omitted. Jesus notices the person others have overlooked and begins a conversation. Bring a long wait to Christ without shame, and seek trustworthy care, practical support, and companionship.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 12:15",
          "John 5:8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 129,
        "title": "May 8",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“He that was healed knew not who it was.”</em></p>\n<p>John 5:13</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Acquaint now yourself with him.”</em></p>\n<p>Job 22:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> This verse meets human fear, grief, illness, or weakness without turning suffering into proof of guilt. Trust in God can coexist with lament, safety planning, medical care, counseling, and the support of a faithful community.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Compassionate God, stay near in suffering, lead me toward wise help, and make me attentive to another person's pain. Amen.</p>",
