      {
        "day_number": 152,
        "title": "May 31",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The king also himself passed over the brook Kidron.”</em></p>\n<p>2 Samuel 15:23</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Second Samuel 15:23 describes David crossing the Kidron while fleeing violence, and the Gospels later place Jesus near the same brook before his arrest. Spurgeon's original reflection says every hardship is God's disciplinary rod; that claim is omitted. Christian faith does not require us to call abuse, illness, bereavement, or poverty divine punishment. Christ meets people in sorrow. Seek safety and support where needed, and accompany someone who is carrying grief.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Who heals all your diseases.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 103:3</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Psalm 103:3 praises God's forgiveness and healing. Spurgeon's original reading repeatedly uses physical disease as a symbol of moral corruption and promises a spiritual cure without relapse; that language is omitted. Illness is not evidence of personal guilt, and people living with chronic conditions are not devotional symbols. We can confess wrongdoing, receive grace, and also seek appropriate health care. Pray for mercy, wise clinicians, caregivers, and all who live with pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "2 Samuel 15:23",
          "Psalm 103:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 153,
        "title": "June 1",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The evening and the morning were the first day.”</em></p>\n<p>Genesis 1:5</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Faith is not denial of evidence, pain, or honest questions. It is a growing reliance on God's character that can seek wisdom, receive help, and take the next faithful step without pretending certainty we do not have.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“He will make her wilderness like Eden.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 51:3</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Genesis 1:5",
          "Isaiah 51:3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 154,
        "title": "June 2",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“For the flesh lusts against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh.”</em></p>\n<p>Galatians 5:17</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Holiness is life being reshaped by communion with God. It appears in integrity, compassion, self-control, justice, and love rather than contempt for people who struggle or confidence in our own superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Good Master.”</em></p>\n<p>Matthew 19:16</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> This verse directs our attention to Jesus Christ, the eternal Son who became truly human for our salvation. Receive what it reveals about his character or work, and let trust in him take visible form in love.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Galatians 5:17",
          "Matthew 19:16"
        ]
      },
