        "day_number": 176,
        "title": "June 24",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“A certain woman of the company lifted up her voice, and said to him, Blessed is the womb that bare you, and the paps which you have sucked. But he said, Yes rather, blessed are they that hear the word of God, and keep it.”</em></p>\n<p>Luke 11:27,28</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> God uses Scripture to teach, correct, comfort, and form a people of love. Read this verse in its biblical context, attend to Christ at the center of Christian faith, and let understanding lead to faithful action.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of truth, open the Scriptures to me, guard me from careless interpretation, and shape my life by your wisdom. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego, answered and said ... Be it known to you, O king, that we will not serve your gods.”</em></p>\n<p>Daniel 3:16,18</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Faithful service joins prayer with responsible action. Offer your time, work, resources, or attention freely and wisely, without using generosity to control others or treating it as a bargain for prosperity.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Generous God, help me serve with humility, respect my real limits, and share what I have for the common good. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Luke 11:27,28",
          "Daniel 3:16,18"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 177,
        "title": "June 25",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Get you up into the high mountain.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 40:9</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“The dove found no rest for the sole of her foot.”</em></p>\n<p>Genesis 8:9</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> In Genesis 8:9 the dove returns because it finds no resting place. The image invites us to consider what offers lasting peace. Rather than using spiritual discomfort to accuse someone of hypocrisy, bring your questions and unmet needs to Christ honestly. Rest is not earned by denying exhaustion, grief, or joy in ordinary life. Make space today for prayer, healthy companionship, and the kind of mercy you would offer a weary friend.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Prince of Peace, make me truthful and courageous in reconciliation, and lead me in ways that protect dignity and life. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 40:9",
          "Genesis 8:9"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 178,
        "title": "June 26",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Are you become like to us?”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 14:10</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Isaiah 14 is a taunt against a fallen oppressor. Spurgeon's original application expands it into an imagined scene of demons publicly tormenting a hypocrite in hell; the threats and speculation are omitted. The passage can still call us to integrity. Where public profession and private conduct conflict, Christ invites truthful confession, protection of anyone harmed, and concrete repair. Examine yourself without imagining or celebrating another person's condemnation.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Having escaped the corruption that is in the world through lust.”</em></p>\n<p>2 Peter 1:4</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Second Peter 1:4 calls believers to share in God's life and turn from destructive desire. Spurgeon's original application uses shaming and fear-filled rhetoric to demand holiness; that rhetoric is omitted. The Holy Spirit forms truthfulness, self-control, compassion, and love without contempt for struggling people. Name one habit that harms you or your neighbor, ask for grace, and seek accountable help for change.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 14:10",
          "2 Peter 1:4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 179,
