        "day_number": 246,
        "title": "September 2",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“But Simon’s wife’s mother lay sick of a fever, and soon they tell him of her.”</em></p>\n<p>Mark 1:30</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> When Peter's mother-in-law was ill, the disciples told Jesus, and he cared for her (Mark 1:30–31). Family responsibilities and service to Christ can belong together. Bring someone who is ill to him in prayer, and offer practical help where you can.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Compassionate God, stay near in suffering, lead me toward wise help, and make me attentive to another person's pain. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Except you see signs and wonders, you will not believe.”</em></p>\n<p>John 4:48</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> In John 4, Jesus challenges a demand that faith depend on spectacular proof and then responds compassionately to a father's plea for his child. Spurgeon's original appeal shames questions and threatens hell in order to force a decision; that coercive rhetoric is omitted. Christian faith may include honest doubt, careful inquiry, and a growing trust in Jesus' character. Bring your questions without pretending, attend to the gospel's witness, and do not manipulate another person's conscience.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Mark 1:30",
          "John 4:48"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 247,
        "title": "September 3",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“You whom my soul loves.”</em></p>\n<p>Song of Solomon 1:7</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Love of God takes shape in patient, truthful care for neighbors, family, strangers, and the church. Healthy love respects conscience and boundaries; it does not excuse harm or demand silence from those who suffer.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of communion, teach me to love with truth, patience, courage, and respect for every person's dignity. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“The Lord tries the righteous.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 11:5</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Holiness is life being reshaped by communion with God. It appears in integrity, compassion, self-control, justice, and love rather than contempt for people who struggle or confidence in our own superiority.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Holy God, conform me to Christ and make my words, habits, and relationships bear the fruit of your love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Song of Solomon 1:7",
          "Psalm 11:5"
        ]
      },
