      {
        "day_number": 270,
        "title": "September 26",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The myrtle trees that were in the bottom.”</em></p>\n<p>Zechariah 1:8</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Howl, fir tree, for the cedar is fallen.”</em></p>\n<p>Zechariah 11:2</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Zechariah 11:2 uses the fall of great trees as an image of public loss. Spurgeon's original reading calls an unprepared person a madman and uses imminent death to pressure the reader; that stigmatizing and fear-based language is omitted. Mortality can invite wise preparation without panic: reconcile where possible, arrange responsibilities, tell the truth, and receive Christ's mercy. Mental illness is not a synonym for folly or unbelief.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Zechariah 1:8",
          "Zechariah 11:2"
        ]
      },
