      {
        "day_number": 280,
        "title": "October 6",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him shall never thirst.”</em></p>\n<p>John 4:14</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Jesus promises living water that becomes a spring of eternal life. Spurgeon's original application suggests that a true believer is continually content and cannot experience a painful thirst for comfort; that claim can burden people living with depression, trauma, loneliness, or prolonged hardship, so it is omitted. Christ remains faithful when joy feels distant. Receive spiritual and practical support, seek appropriate mental-health care, and let the church carry hope with those who cannot feel it today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“He had married an Ethiopian woman.”</em></p>\n<p>Numbers 12:1</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Numbers 12 reports that Miriam and Aaron spoke against Moses because of his Cushite wife, and the narrative rebukes their opposition. Spurgeon's original reflection calls the marriage strange and uses Blackness and Ethiopian identity as symbols of sin and unworthiness; that racialized imagery is rejected and omitted. Ethnicity and skin color are not moral conditions. Honor the dignity of every people, confront racism in Christian language and practice, and receive Christ's grace without degrading anyone's identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Trustworthy God, hold me amid uncertainty and guide my next step in wisdom, courage, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "John 4:14",
          "Numbers 12:1"
        ]
      },
