      {
        "day_number": 329,
        "title": "November 24",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“The glorious Lord will be to us a place of broad rivers and streams.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 33:21</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Isaiah 33:21 pictures the Lord as a place of broad rivers, security, and abundance. Spurgeon's original reading says that any unmet need is the sufferer's own fault; that claim is omitted. God's presence does not erase material need or justify blaming people who lack food, housing, care, or safety. Receive God's refuge, name your needs honestly, and help the church become a place where resources and burdens are shared.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Yet a little sleep, a little slumber, a little folding of the hands to sleep: so shall your poverty come as one that travels; and your want as an armed man.”</em></p>\n<p>Proverbs 24:33,34</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Proverbs 24:33–34 warns that repeated neglect can overtake a field and household. It does not teach that every experience of poverty comes from laziness, nor does it require terror about a missed spiritual deadline. Economic hardship has many causes, and healthy rest is not moral failure. Consider one responsibility you have postponed, take a manageable step, and extend practical compassion rather than judgment to people under strain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of wisdom, give me a teachable heart, truthful speech, and discernment that produces peace and justice. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Isaiah 33:21",
          "Proverbs 24:33,34"
        ]
      },
