        "day_number": 336,
        "title": "December 1",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“You have made summer and winter.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 74:17</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Psalm 74:17 praises God as Lord of the seasons. Spurgeon's original application says that losses, illness, and poverty are all sent by God with a wise design; that certainty about another person's suffering is omitted. We may trust God in hardship without pretending to know its cause or calling harm good. Ask for comfort, seek practical help and safety, and stand with someone whose difficult season has lasted longer than expected.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“O that men would praise the Lord for his goodness, and for his wonderful works to the children of men.”</em></p>\n<p>Psalm 107:8</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Psalm 107 repeatedly thanks God for steadfast love and deliverance. Spurgeon's original application suggests that complaining less will necessarily make us happier and centers gratitude on abilities not everyone possesses. Scripture also makes room for lament. Gratitude need not silence grief, disability, anger, or unmet need. Name one gift with thanks and one pain with honesty, then ask how you can share God's steadfast love with another person.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Father, Son, and Holy Spirit, receive my worship and make my whole life an offering of gratitude, truth, and love. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Psalm 74:17",
          "Psalm 107:8"
        ]
      },
