        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Orpah kissed her mother in law; but Ruth clave to her.”</em></p>\n<p>Ruth 1:14</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Ruth 1:14 records that Orpah tearfully returned to her people while Ruth stayed with Naomi. Scripture does not tell us that Orpah's remaining years were godless or wasted; those judgments are Spurgeon's speculation and are omitted here. Ruth's steadfast care remains a beautiful example, but this story should also teach us to avoid declaring the worth of a person's life from one difficult decision. Offer loyal care where you can, and grant others dignity when their circumstances differ from yours.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of communion, teach me to love with truth, patience, courage, and respect for every person's dignity. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“And lay your foundations with sapphires.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 54:11</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Ruth 1:14",
          "Isaiah 54:11"
        ]
      },
      {
        "day_number": 351,
        "title": "December 16",
        "content": "<p>Morning</p>\n<p><em>“Come to me.”</em></p>\n<p>Matthew 11:28</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial morning reflection:</strong> Matthew 11:28–30 records Jesus' invitation to those who are weary and burdened. Spurgeon contrasts this kindness with a negative portrayal of Jewish law, but the Hebrew Scriptures themselves speak repeatedly of God's compassion, mercy, and welcome (Psalm 103:8–13). Christians need not disparage Judaism to celebrate Jesus' gentleness. Bring a burden to Christ, and look for ways to lighten someone else's burden without criticizing their religious identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more before the day gathers pace. Name one concrete way its truth can shape your attention, speech, or action today.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> Lord Jesus Christ, true God and true human, draw me into your life and teach me to follow you in love. Amen.</p>\n<p>Evening</p>\n<p><em>“Yes, you heard not; yes, you knew not; yes, from that time that your ear was not opened.”</em></p>\n<p>Isaiah 48:8</p>\n<p><strong>Editorial evening reflection:</strong> Biblical hope is patient confidence in God's faithfulness, not a promise that every difficulty will quickly disappear. Let hope make room for honest lament and sustain one concrete act of love today.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Read the verse once more and review the day without denial or self-contempt. Give thanks, name what needs repair, and entrust tomorrow to God.</p>\n<p><strong>Prayer:</strong> God of hope, keep me faithful while I wait and let your promised future strengthen love in the present. Amen.</p>",
        "references": [
          "Matthew 11:28",
          "Isaiah 48:8"
        ]
      },
